Retrieval-augmented generation has a default architecture now: chunk the
documents, embed the chunks, put them in a vector database, retrieve the top-k
by cosine similarity, stuff them into a prompt.

It works. It is also frequently a worse answer than a `WHERE` clause, and the
failure is hard to see from the inside — because a vector search always returns
something. There is no empty result set to tell you the approach was wrong. You
get five plausible-looking chunks and an LLM confidently summarising them.

## The question to ask first

**Does the user's query express a fuzzy concept, or a precise filter?**

"What did we agree about payment terms?" is fuzzy. The words in the contract may
be "net thirty", "remittance", or "settlement period", none of which appear in
the question. Lexical search fails. Embeddings are the right tool.

"Show me invoices over ₹50,000 from last quarter that are unpaid" is not fuzzy at
all. It is three predicates and a sort. Embedding it produces chunks that are
*about* large unpaid invoices, which is not the same as the correct set, and the
LLM will happily present the approximation as the answer.

The tell is whether a competent person with SQL access could answer the question
exactly. If they could, retrieval that is merely similar is a downgrade dressed
as intelligence.

{% callout %}
Most real product questions are a mix. "Which unpaid invoices mention a
disputed delivery?" is a SQL filter *and* a semantic search. Answering it well
means doing both — filter first, then search inside the result.
{% /callout %}

## Filter first, then search

That last case is where naive RAG loses badly, because filtering after retrieval
means you searched the wrong corpus. If you retrieve the top 20 semantically
similar chunks and *then* filter to unpaid invoices, you may be left with two —
not because only two match, but because the other matches ranked 21st onward.

Pre-filtering is why I mostly reach for pgvector now.

```sql
-- The filter runs first. The vector search happens inside the rows that
-- already satisfy the business predicate.
SELECT
  c.id,
  c.content,
  c.embedding <=> $1 AS distance
FROM invoice_chunk c
JOIN invoice i ON i.id = c.invoice_id
WHERE i.tenant_id = $2
  AND i.status    = 'UNPAID'
  AND i.issued_at >= $3
ORDER BY c.embedding <=> $1
LIMIT 10;
```

That query is doing something a dedicated vector store makes genuinely awkward:
joining embeddings to relational state that changes independently of them. An
invoice's status changes; its embedding does not. Keeping them in the same
database means the filter is always current and always consistent, with no
synchronisation job to drift.

## Pinecone versus pgvector, honestly

I have run both in production. The decision is less about the vectors than about
where the rest of your truth lives.

**pgvector** when your embeddings are attached to relational data you already
store in Postgres, when queries need to filter on that data, and when scale is
in the low millions of vectors. One database, transactional consistency between
a row and its embedding, no sync pipeline, and no second thing to pay for and
monitor. This covers a large majority of application RAG.

**Pinecone** when the vector workload is genuinely the primary workload — tens of
millions of vectors and up, high query concurrency, and you would rather not own
index tuning, replication, and the memory characteristics of HNSW. Managed
infrastructure is a real product and there is a scale at which building it
yourself is a bad use of your time.

The failure mode I would push back on hardest is reaching for a managed vector
database at 50,000 chunks. At that size pgvector on the Postgres instance you
already run is faster to build, cheaper, easier to reason about, and removes an
entire class of consistency bug.

```sql
-- HNSW: better recall/latency than IVFFlat, and no training step.
CREATE INDEX ON invoice_chunk
  USING hnsw (embedding vector_cosine_ops)
  WITH (m = 16, ef_construction = 64);

-- Raise at query time when recall matters more than latency.
SET hnsw.ef_search = 100;
```

## Chunking is where the quality is

More time goes here than into choosing a vector store, and it has more effect on
output quality than the embedding model does.

**Split on structure, not character count.** A 512-token window that cuts a table
in half produces a chunk that is retrievable and useless. Split on headings,
clauses, list boundaries — whatever the document's own structure is. Fall back to
token windows only where there is no structure to use.

**Carry context into the chunk.** A chunk reading "This does not apply to
enterprise accounts" is meaningless in isolation. Prefixing each chunk with its
document title and heading path — `Contract 4412 › Payment Terms › Exceptions` —
costs a few tokens and measurably improves both retrieval and the model's
ability to use what it retrieved.

**Overlap, modestly.** Ten to fifteen percent catches sentences that straddle a
boundary. More than that inflates your index and returns near-duplicate chunks
that crowd out genuinely different results.

## Evaluate retrieval separately from generation

When a RAG answer is wrong, there are two possible causes: the right chunk was
never retrieved, or it was retrieved and the model ignored it. These have
completely different fixes, and you cannot tell them apart by reading the answer.

Keep a small set — thirty is enough to start — of real questions with the chunk
ids that should be retrieved. Measure recall@k on retrieval alone. When quality
regresses, you immediately know which half moved. Almost every RAG quality
problem I have chased was a retrieval problem being debugged as a prompting
problem.

## The uncomfortable summary

Semantic search is a genuinely good answer to a narrower question than the
current enthusiasm suggests. Before building it, try to answer the user's actual
questions with SQL and see how far you get. Where SQL is sufficient it will be
faster, cheaper, exactly correct, and explainable.

Where it is not sufficient — where the user's words and the document's words do
not overlap — embeddings do something nothing else does. That is worth building
carefully. It is just not worth building by default.
